    "title": "Travelers at Springfield-Branson Airport react to government shutdown's impact on TSA - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Michael McLaughlin ,\n1 hours ago SPRINGFIELD, Mo. — As the federal government shutdown continues, travelers at Springfield-Branson National Airport are voicing concerns about how Transportation Security Administration (TSA) employees are being affected.\nBob Kern, who was returning to Florida with his wife, said he only learned about the shutdown the night before his flight. While he wasn’t worried about the immediate impact, he said the situation could worsen if the standoff drags on.\n“I feel that if it goes on for more than five days, then there’ll be problems,” Kern said. “I don’t think there’s going to be a problem the first day.”\nAnother traveler, Susan Brown, said she noticed signs of stress among TSA workers during her security screening.\n“You can definitely see that they were not in a good mood today,” Brown said. “The people that are not getting paid are the ones who worked hardest and the ones who work the longest hours and spend the most time away from their families.”\nBrown added that an extended shutdown could result in flight delays if TSA workers begin to seek other jobs.\nA spokesperson for Springfield-Branson National Airport said no disruptions are expected at this time and passengers should not see changes to their travel plans.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KOLR - OzarksFirst.com.",
